<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/advanced/historical-linguistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Everything you have learned so far — phonology, morphology, syntax — described languages as static
    systems. Historical linguistics adds the dimension of time, and its central discovery is that language
    change is <strong>rule-governed, not random</strong>. That single fact is what makes the impossible look
    routine: comparing the vocabularies of living languages with mathematical regularity, linguists have
    reconstructed Proto-Indo-European — a language spoken before writing, never recorded by anyone, now known
    to thousands of words — and built verified family trees for every continent. This lesson works the
    machinery: the sound laws, the comparative method, the types of change, and the method's known limits.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Grimm's law, the comparative method, regularity, and the types of change" widget="equationStep" config={histCfg} />

  <h2>Sound change and its regularity: Grimm and Verner</h2>
  <p>
    Jakob Grimm (1822) noticed that Latin and English cognates correspond not randomly but
    <strong>systematically</strong>: Latin <em>pater, pes, piscis</em> against English <em>father, foot,
    fish</em> — p to f every time; <em>tres</em> against <em>three</em> — t to th; <em>cornu, canis</em>
    against <em>horn, hound</em> — k to h. Grimm's law is a chain shift that happened once, in the ancestor
    of Germanic: PIE voiceless stops p, t, k became the fricatives f, θ, h; voiced b, d, g became p, t, k;
    voiced aspirates bh, dh, gh became b, d, g. Because it ran over the <em>whole inventory</em> — every
    word, no exceptions — the correspondences are evidence of common descent, not resemblance. English
    "pen" and Latin "penna" do not match because of borrowing; they match because both descend from one
    sound that changed along one rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    But there was a famous hitch: <em>brother</em> (Latin <em>frāter</em>, t to th) follows Grimm, while
    <em>father</em> (Latin <em>pater</em>, but t to <strong>d</strong>) seems to break it. In 1877 Karl
    Verner showed the "exception" was another law: the resulting fricatives voiced when the PIE accent fell
    on a <em>later</em> syllable — PIE <em>pətḗr</em> (accent after the t) gave Germanic <em>fadēr</em>,
    while <em>bhrā́ter</em> (accent before the t) kept <em>þ</em>. One paradox became two regular laws —
    the discipline's founding demonstration that <strong>exceptions to sound laws are other laws, analogy,
    or borrowing</strong>. This is the Neogrammarian hypothesis: sound change is exceptionless within its
    environment. It remains the working assumption of the field, with a known complication (some changes
    spread word by word — lexical diffusion — as Labov's work and the next lesson show).
  </p>

  <h2>The comparative method: reconstruction as inference</h2>
  <p>
    The comparative method is an engine for turning correspondences into a proto-language. The recipe:
    assemble <strong>cognate sets</strong> — words in related languages that descend from the same ancestor,
    <em>not</em> borrowings — tabulate the correspondences, and reconstruct the ancestral sound that best
    explains the pattern. Worked miniature. Compare "father" forms: Sanskrit <em>pitar-</em>, Greek
    <em>patḗr</em>, Latin <em>pater</em>, Gothic <em>fadar</em>. The correspondence p : p : p : f
    repeats across dozens of sets (<em>pes/foot, piscis/fish, per/for</em>), so the safest inference is a
    proto-consonant *p, preserved as p in most branches and shifted to f in Germanic. Directionality is
    argued from markedness (fricative from stop is vastly more common than the reverse) and from internal
    evidence — and it can be <em>tested</em>. Saussure reconstructed "coefficient sonantiques" (laryngeals)
    for PIE purely from vowel-pattern regularities; decades later Hittite was deciphered and turned out to
    preserve those consonants as ḫ, exactly where predicted — Kuryłowicz announced the confirmation in 1927.
    A dead language partly verified by a prediction: no other human science reconstructs its unobservable
    past with this precision.
  </p>
  <p>
    The output for Indo-European: a reconstructed vocabulary revealing a pastoral, patriarchal,
    horse-and-wheel people (the words for horse, wheel, axle, and kinship are all reconstructible; the words
    for "sea" are less secure), plausibly the steppe communities dated around 4500–2500 BCE — a picture that
    ancient-DNA studies of Yamnaya remains have since independently corroborated (the steppe expansion into
    Europe, Haak and colleagues, 2015). Across the world the same method maps the great families:
    Indo-European (first language of roughly 46% of humanity), Sino-Tibetan, Niger-Congo, Afroasiatic, and
    Austronesian — whose reconstructed tree matches the archaeology of a spread from Taiwan to Madagascar
    and Easter Island, the widest pre-modern dispersal known.
  </p>

  <h2>The types of change</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Sound change</strong> is the engine (Grimm; Verner; assimilation, lenition), and English
    carries its most teachable exhibit: the <strong>Great Vowel Shift</strong> (c. 1400–1700), which raised
    every long vowel one step — Middle English <em>mice</em> (mees) and <em>mouse</em> (moos) became today's
    diphthongs /aɪ/ and /aʊ/. This is why English spelling is "chaotic": it froze just before the shift
    finished, and "where" no longer predicts "how." <strong>Analogy</strong> is the other great force:
    irregular forms regularise on productive patterns — Old English <em>holp</em> became <em>helped</em>,
    <em>kine</em> became <em>cows</em>; children's "goed" is the same mechanism alive in the nursery.
    <strong>Borrowing</strong> redistributes vocabulary: after 1066, French supplied the vocabulary of
    government, law and cuisine (well over half of English dictionary entries are Latinate, while the
    hundred commonest words remain Germanic — the word "beef" from French, the "cow" it names from English,
    in one lexical stratum). <strong>Semantic drift</strong> quietly rewrites meanings: <em>silly</em> once
    meant blessed, <em>nice</em> meant foolish, <em>awful</em> meant awe-inspiring. And
    <strong>grammaticalisation</strong> — the deepest channel: content words grind down into grammar
    (English "going to" → "gonna" as a pure future marker; the same path produced Romance futures from Latin
    infinitive + "have").
  </p>

  <h2>Trees, waves, and the method's limits</h2>
  <p>
    The family-tree model (Schleicher, 1860s) captures descent by splitting, but real change also spreads
    <strong>geographically as a wave</strong> (Johannes Schmidt) across dialect continua — hence the
    distinction between a <em>family tree</em> of languages and a <em>wave model</em> of innovations, needed
    jointly. The limits are equally instructive. The comparative method reaches roughly 8,000–10,000 years
    before reconstructed signals blur: proposals for macrofamilies beyond that (Nostratic, Altaic) remain
    unproven, and mass comparison à la Greenberg lacks the regularity that gives the method its power. The
    dating problem is live: Swadesh's mid-century glottochronology assumed a constant replacement rate and
    failed; modern Bayesian phylogenetics (Gray and Atkinson, 2003) dated PIE deep (~8,000–9,500 years,
    supporting an Anatolian farming spread) — and the steppe hypothesis counter-argued with archaeology and
    ancient DNA for a later date (~5,500 years). The debate is a model of science at its edge: two data
    streams, explicit models, unresolved.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> some languages "evolve" while others stagnate — that written languages
  like French or Sanskrit are fixed. Every language changes at every level, continuously; prestige writing
  systems merely change their <em>standard</em>, which is itself renegotiated generation by generation. The
  linguistics lesson's core fact applies to the languages of power most of all: change is happening to
  English right now, in your own speech, invisibly.</p>

  <h2>Grimm's law as a system: the full correspondence, worked</h2>
  <p>
    Grimm's law is three parallel shifts, and you should be able to run each column with examples. The
    voiceless stops: PIE <em>p, t, k</em> → Proto-Germanic <em>f, þ, h</em> — Latin <em>pater</em> /
    English <em>father</em>; Latin <em>tres</em> / <em>three</em>; Latin <em>cornu</em> / <em>horn</em>,
    <em>canis</em> / <em>hound</em>. The voiced stops: <em>b, d, g</em> → <em>p, t, k</em> — rarer in
    attested material (few classical languages kept plain *b), but <em>g</em> → <em>k</em> shows in
    Greek <em>genos</em> against English <em>kin</em>. The voiced aspirates: <em>bh, dh, gh</em> →
    <em>b, d, g</em> — Sanskrit <em>bhrā́tar-</em> / <em>brother</em>; Sanskrit <em>rudhira-</em> (red) /
    English <em>red</em> shares the d-g column's reflexes; Latin <em>hostis</em> (stranger, enemy) /
    English <em>guest</em>. Two further disciplines complete the picture. <strong>Chain shifts keep the
    system balanced</strong>: when p emptied into f, b slid into the vacated p slot, and bh into b's — a
    push chain that explains why the shift looks "designed." <strong>Exceptions are diagnosable</strong>:
    Latin <em>spuere</em> / <em>spit</em> keeps p after s (fricatives did not shift after fricatives);
    <em>ped-</em> survives as p in <em>pedal</em> because it arrived by borrowing from Latin, long after
    the shift — the comparative method distinguishes inherited words from learned ones, which is why
    English has both <em>fatherly</em> and <em>paternal</em> for the same root.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact, borrowing, and beyond the tree</h2>
  <p>
    The family tree is only half the model, because languages also change through <strong>contact</strong>.
    Borrowing is stratified by prestige and domain (English's Norse layer is everyday words — skirt and
    shirt are the same Norse/English word twice — while the French layer is law and cuisine);
    <strong>substratum</strong> effects leave conquerors' languages speaking with the conquered's accent
    and grammar; and <strong>sprachbund</strong> (areal convergence) makes unrelated neighbours resemble
    each other — the Balkan languages share a future construction and clitic patterns no common ancestor
    explains. At the extreme, <strong>mixed languages</strong> crystallise in bilingual communities:
    Michif combines Cree verb morphology with French noun phrases — not a creole, but two grammars
    fused by choice. The methodological upshot is the field's central caution: shared vocabulary proves
    contact; only <em>regular sound correspondences in core vocabulary</em> (and shared irregular
    morphology — the strongest evidence of all, since nobody borrows a paradigm like good/better/best)
    prove common descent. Family trees are drawn in inherited irregularity.
  </p>

  <h2>Semantic change: the typology, worked</h2>
  <p>
    Meaning change has its own taxonomy, and every category has an English exhibit.
    <strong>Broadening</strong>: <em>dog</em> once named a specific powerful breed (the generic word was
    <em>hund</em>, surviving as hound) — now the specific name ate the generic slot. <strong>Narrowing</strong>:
    <em>deer</em> was the ordinary word for any animal (cognate with German <em>Tier</em>) before
    specialising. <strong>Amelioration</strong>: <em>knight</em> was a servant boy; <em>pretty</em> meant
    cunning or crafty. <strong>Pejoration</strong>: <em>silly</em> went blessed → innocent → foolish — a
    classic downward slide (note how often the "feminine" words pejorate: <em>hussy</em> was housewife,
    <em>mistress</em> became a loaded term while <em>master</em> did not — social hierarchy writing
    itself into etymology). <strong>Metaphorical extension</strong> is the engine of ordinary vocabulary:
    <em>grasp</em>, <em>see</em>, <em>run</em> (a program runs; a nose runs), <em>deadline</em> (a literal
    line prisoners were shot crossing). The systematicity matters: senses drift along predictable paths
    (concrete → abstract, body-part → spatial: the "head" of a bed, "foot" of a mountain) so regularly
    that reconstruction uses semantic correspondences alongside sound correspondences — and a candidate
    cognate pair whose meanings drift through no known pathway is suspect, whatever its phonetics.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three research frontiers connect this lesson to the rest of your ladder. <strong>Sociolinguistics of
    change</strong> (Labov's department-store study, the Martha's Vineyard vowels) showed sound change can be
    observed <em>in progress</em>, tracked to social identity — history happening in real time, which the
    next lesson formalises. <strong>Computational phylogenetics</strong> is transforming the field: Bayesian
    models over Swadesh-style wordlists now date families (Austronesian, Sino-Tibetan, IE) and test tree
    versus wave transmissions quantitatively. <strong>Punctuated equilibria versus gradualism</strong> (Dixon's
    controversial model) asks whether languages drift slowly and shift abruptly at contact events — an open
    empirical question. The deepest open problems: why some grammatical features (SOV order, numeral systems)
    are strikingly stable over millennia while lexicons race; whether change has direction (are analytic
    languages "later" than synthetic ones — the cyclical theories say yes, with caveats); and whether the
    roughly 7,000 languages' diversity is contraction-bound — historical linguistics' grim demographic
    corollary, since reconstruction needs descendants, and the descendants are vanishing faster than the
    method can work.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Grimm, <em>Deutsche Grammatik</em> (1822); Campbell, <em>Historical Linguistics: An Introduction</em>; Anthony, <em>The Horse, the Wheel, and Language</em> (2007); Haak et al. (2015); Gray &amp; Atkinson, <em>Nature</em> (2003); Labov, <em>Principles of Linguistic Change</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
